01:19:46.680 --> 01:19:53.000
<v Mike>So you better believe that we'll do, um, earth bound and I don't know, Shin Megami Tensai

01:19:53.000 --> 01:19:54.740
<v Mike>because I believe it started there as well.

01:19:54.800 --> 01:19:56.960
<v Mike>So we'll just get to the beginning of every franchise eventually.

01:19:57.340 --> 01:20:02.380
<v Mike>Just like every episode eventually gets to the essential games list.

01:20:05.700 --> 01:20:11.640
<v Mike>I said it earlier in the episode, but I truly believe that Star Tropics is what people feared

01:20:11.640 --> 01:20:13.920
<v Mike>Zelda would be before Breath of the Wild.

01:20:14.120 --> 01:20:20.460
<v Mike>An incredibly linear experience where everything is so streamlined that the next place you approach

01:20:20.460 --> 01:20:23.400
<v Mike>or room in a dungeon is always forward progress.

01:20:23.700 --> 01:20:29.700
<v Mike>There's tons of set dressing to distract you, but everything is planned down to the dialogue.

01:20:29.700 --> 01:20:37.440
<v Mike>Somehow, even if that sounds scathing, none of that mattered during my time playing this game

01:20:37.440 --> 01:20:46.960
<v Mike>and any sort of, uh, flaws or qualms I might have with this game, it is impossible to deny

01:20:46.960 --> 01:20:51.340
<v Mike>that, uh, this is one of the great games on the NES.

01:20:51.880 --> 01:20:56.480
<v Mike>Uh, is it a little limited by, uh, the time that it was made?

01:20:56.480 --> 01:21:04.040
<v Mike>Yes, but in terms of other NES games we've played, few have had an adventure this grand.

01:21:04.040 --> 01:21:08.440
<v Mike>And I know that that's funny because we were talking about how this story is just kind of whatever.

01:21:08.620 --> 01:21:11.880
<v Mike>I don't really feel that the story is just whatever.

01:21:11.880 --> 01:21:15.040
<v Mike>I think that, uh, it's silly.

01:21:15.040 --> 01:21:16.000
<v Mike>It's pulpy.

01:21:16.260 --> 01:21:21.980
<v Mike>It's a chapter of the month style adventuring, but it is an adventure nevertheless.

01:21:21.980 --> 01:21:29.940
<v Mike>And I really enjoyed, uh, this journey both from, uh, the gameplay side, from the story side,

01:21:29.940 --> 01:21:33.420
<v Mike>and just seeing, like, what they would do next.

01:21:33.420 --> 01:21:40.960
<v Mike>Even if it is very linear, that provides a focused experience that creates a lot of memorable moments

01:21:40.960 --> 01:21:48.680
<v Mike>like, uh, the piano and figuring out the, uh, the Do, Re, Mi code in order to open up the next gate.

01:21:49.360 --> 01:21:56.940
<v Mike>There are certainly, uh, things in this game that, uh, bothered me at first that I even eventually,

01:21:57.140 --> 01:21:59.340
<v Mike>like, came around on, such as the movement.

01:21:59.340 --> 01:22:05.120
<v Mike>It was something that I thought was going to be, like, well, this might hold it off the list while I was playing.

01:22:05.280 --> 01:22:06.840
<v Mike>But in the end, it didn't matter.

01:22:06.840 --> 01:22:12.140
<v Mike>And I still made it all the way through this game, not in spite of it, but alongside it.

01:22:12.340 --> 01:22:15.600
<v Mike>So, for me, this is an obvious essential games list choice.

01:22:16.100 --> 01:22:19.840
<v Joe>Joe? I, I'll just come right out and say I completely agree.

01:22:20.100 --> 01:22:21.940
<v Joe>Uh, this, I, I love this game.

01:22:22.020 --> 01:22:23.640
<v Joe>I mean, yeah. Or are there flaws?

01:22:23.860 --> 01:22:26.120
